WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.660
Despite their vast differences in style and
tone throughout the decades, every Batman

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:08.860
movie tends to pull from the same handful
of references and plot points again and again,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.080
to the point where you can pretty much set
your bat-watch by them.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.080
Hey, did you know Bruce Wayne's parents were
shot by a mugger in front of him when he was

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:16.580
a kid?

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.840
If you didn't, it's safe to assume that you've
never seen a movie about the Dark Knight,

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.360
because it comes up a lot.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:26.420
Seriously, we've had more scenes of Thomas
and Martha Wayne being gunned down in an alley

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:28.220
over the years than we've had Batmobiles.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:30.820
Admittedly, that makes a lot of sense.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:35.000
Along with the bats that inspired Bruce to
put on a costume in order to strike fear into

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:39.590
the superstitious, cowardly lot that is Gotham
City's criminal underworld, it's the single

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:41.480
most important thing about his origin story.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:45.860
Still, once you've seen it happen for the
fifth or sixth time, it starts to lose its

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:48.600
impact, even if each version tries to add
something new.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:54.040
Tim Burton's 1989 Batman film makes it a major
plot point by casting the murderer as the

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:55.900
crook who would eventually become the Joker.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:00.160
Batman Forever shows the murders multiple
times as part of a story where the Riddler

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:01.300
is stealing memories.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.800
Batman Begins replaces the traditional movie
theater with an opera house, and even shows

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:10.680
us the trial of a remorseful Joe Chill, something
that's never really been done in another Batman

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:11.180
story.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:17.260
"Your honor, not a day goes by that I don’t
wish I could take back what I did."

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:22.400
Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice leans into
slow-motion close-ups, and puts a spin on

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.880
it by having bats burst out of Martha Wayne's
tomb.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:30.000
The one Batman film that doesn't feature the
Wayne murders is, of course, Batman: The Movie,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.400
which ties into the 1966 Adam West TV series.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:38.840
In fact, in the show's entire 120-episode
run, plus the movie, the killing is only ever

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.540
mentioned once, in the pilot.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:44.940
"Perhaps if there had been anti-crime centers
of the type you now propose when my own parents

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.920
were murdered by dastardly criminals."

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:51.290
It seems like Warner Bros. finally made up
for that in 2018, when they included what

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:55.810
might just be the darkest — and most hilarious
— take on the Wayne murders in the kid-friendly

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:57.700
Teen Titans Go to the Movies.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.940
Who knew Robin was that stone cold?

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:05.080
More than any other superhero, Batman has
an incredible roster of compelling villains,

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:09.890
with a deep bench that goes from worldwide
threats like Ra's al-Ghul to sinister nuisances

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:14.340
like the Riddler, and even classic antiheroes
like Catwoman, who have become main characters

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.160
worthy of a spotlight in their own right.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.440
They're the best bad guys in comics.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:22.860
As a result, the Batman movies are always
eager to introduce a new villain, and almost

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.760
all of them make sure to dive into their origin
stories.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:33.200
"Jack is dead my friend, you can call me Joker."

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:38.590
The big exception is, of course, Batman '66,
where all four baddies are already well-known

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:40.880
arch-criminals, just like they are on the
show.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:43.660
The rest of them, however, go hard at origins.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:48.660
Batman '89 doesn't just show us the Joker
going into the vat of acid, it gives us his

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:53.520
complete backstory as the underling of a mob
boss, complete with its own web of double-crosses.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.940
"You are my number one guy."

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.400
Batman Returns does even more.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:07.260
Not only do we see Selina Kyle's breakdown
and re-emergence as Catwoman, we also get

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.560
the Penguin's literal life story, from infancy
to death.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:15.560
Batman Begins shows us the Scarecrow's transition
from weirdo doctor to full-on supervillain.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:16.860
And Batman &amp; Robin?

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:22.300
That movie actually does this four times,
giving us the origin of Poison Ivy, A backstory

00:03:22.319 --> 00:03:26.220
for Mr. Freeze that explains why he looks
like a bodybuilder, a beginning for Bane,

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:30.800
and if you want to get technical about it,
it even has an origin story for Jason Woodrue,

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.180
the Floronic Man.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:36.840
It's easy to point to The Dark Knight as a
movie that studiously avoids giving its major

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:41.540
villain a backstory, as the Joker gives a
few conflicting versions of his past to preserve

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:42.320
his mystery.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.040
Instead, it's a film focused on the origin
of supervillainy itself.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:50.720
In addition to giving us a full origin for
Two-Face, it also shows the Joker's first

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:54.950
reign of terror in Gotham City, which ends
up being the end of traditional organized

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:57.540
crime and a beginning for costumed supercriminals.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:02.580
For decades, Alfred Pennyworth was Batman's
loyal and somewhat bumbling butler.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:07.300
With a few notable exceptions, however, the
movies tend to avoid introducing Robin, and

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:10.740
Alfred became one of the only people that
Batman can speak to casually.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:15.760
As a result, he became a sarcastic comedic
foil for the very serious Dark Knight.

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:19.960
The clearest example of this is probably Michael
Caine's portrayal of Alfred in the Dark Knight

00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:24.350
trilogy, who seems to delight in throwing
shade at Batman, and pretty much everyone

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:24.840
else.

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:26.800
"Did you get mauled by a tiger?”

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:27.300
“It was a dog.”

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:28.000
“Huh?”

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:29.780
"It was a big dog."

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:32.820
It's definitely present across all versions,
though.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:37.550
The Jeremy Irons version from Batman v Superman
is the only counterbalance to the movie's

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:39.540
otherwise all-consuming grimness.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:43.750
Michael Gough's Alfred revels in dunking on
the Caped Crusader by telling embarrassing

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:46.780
childhood stories about Bruce Wayne to his
potential girlfriend.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:49.620
C'mon, man, let the guy have a couple of secrets!

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:53.180
Batman's identity is one of his most important
secrets.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:57.970
Bruce Wayne is, after all, a very public figure
whose home address is pretty easy to figure

00:04:57.970 --> 00:05:02.240
out, so if his enemies ever discovered exactly
who it was underneath that mask, he'd be in

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:03.220
a whole lot of trouble.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:08.280
And there's also the fact that being a vigilante
is technically extremely illegal, no matter

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.430
how many custom-made spotlights you mount
on top of the police station.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:15.840
And yet, every time Batman hits the silver
screen, the whole "secret" part of his identity

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:17.120
goes right out the window.

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:21.100
The Dark Knight trilogy might actually be
the most egregious in this regard.

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:25.470
Ra's al-Ghul knew from the very beginning,
Rachel Dawes figures it out thanks to Batman

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:29.750
repeating a familiar phrase, and even some
random dude who works at Wayne Enterprises

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:33.780
notices that Batman's exclusively using stuff
that should be down in the warehouse.

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:39.020
In Batman '89, Alfred just walks Vicki Vale
down to the Batcave one afternoon, which not

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:43.540
only blows his secret identity, but also forces
her to see the unforgettable sight of Batman

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:44.680
wearing mom jeans.

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:49.100
In Batman Forever, the Riddler learns it by
literally stealing Bruce Wayne's memories

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.660
and then uses the information to blow up the
Batcave.

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:55.740
Justice League even features a scene where
the extremely famous Bruce Wayne is just hanging

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:58.960
out in public with Aquaman, talking about
being Batman.

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:02.840
All of that can be forgiven, though, because
it also gives us the single greatest moment

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:06.420
of Batman Returns, and one of the best scenes
in any Batman movie.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:11.300
When Batman pulls off his mask to make a heartfelt
plea to Catwoman, Max Shreck — played by

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:14.440
Christopher Walken — reacts the way most
of us would in that moment.

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:16.160
“Why are you dressed up like Batman?"

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:19.760
“Because he IS Batman, you moron."

